WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.060
The line went live last week,

2
00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:04.480
and the support tickets
split into two neat piles.

3
00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:07.040
Quiet callers are fine.
Loud callers hear a harsh,

4
00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.900
raspy buzz on top of their voice.

5
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.540
One says his boss sounds like
he's yelling through a kazoo.

6
00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:15.630
Strange, isn't it? The line runs
the same code for everybody,

7
00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:17.210
and louder should just be louder.

8
00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:19.930
So look at what it carries:
the microphone's voltage,

9
00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:21.770
measured eight thousand times a second.

10
00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.830
Each measurement is a sample, and
a call is a list of numbers.

11
00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:28.770
The simplest sound is a tone,
like a tuning fork: a steady wave,

12
00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.730
and how many times a second
it repeats, in hertz,

13
00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.770
is the pitch you hear.
Its samples come from the cosine.

14
00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:35.030
For one thousand hertz,

15
00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:38.430
the angle steps three hundred sixty
times one thousand over eight thousand.

16
00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:40.530
That's forty-five degrees each sample,

17
00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.630
so after eight samples
the pattern starts over.

18
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.120
Early in the chain sits the booster.

19
00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.420
It's a system:
a rule that takes in one signal,

20
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.280
one list of numbers,
and gives back another.

21
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:52.740
Its rule is one line of code:
multiply every sample by four.

22
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.580
But the chip after it can only
store values from minus one to plus

23
00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.700
one. Hand it two,
and it writes down one.

24
00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.160
That flattening is called clipping.

25
00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:02.000
Take a quiet caller
at size point two,

26
00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.300
so their biggest sample is point two,
and a loud one at point five.

27
00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:06.420
Which comes out clean?

28
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:09.180
The quiet one grows to point
eight and stays inside the range.

29
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.080
The loud one would reach two,
so its tops are sliced off flat.

30
00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:14.630
Is that just an uglier tone?

31
00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:16.290
Try a pencil test. In any tone,

32
00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.350
a sample's two neighbors add up to
the middle sample times a fixed number,

33
00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.190
here one point four one four.

34
00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.110
The clean tone passes.
The clipped one fails.

35
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:26.100
So what is it? It's two tones.

36
00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:27.420
A one thousand hertz tone,

37
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.640
minus a three thousand
hertz tone a sixth its size,

38
00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.660
lands exactly on every clipped sample.

39
00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.300
That second tone is the buzz.

40
00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:35.900
Nobody said it. The booster
made it, out of nothing.

41
00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:38.580
What did the booster break?

42
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:41.580
A system is linear when doubling
the input doubles the output,

43
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:43.700
and two inputs added
give their outputs added.

44
00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.440
The real booster is not linear.

45
00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:48.800
It only behaves like the linear times
four while its inputs stay inside the

46
00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:49.820
range.

47
00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:52.880
And here's the trap. Two quiet
voices share a speakerphone.

48
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:54.860
Each alone comes out
at point eight, fine.

49
00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:57.520
Linearity says together they
should give one point six.

50
00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.400
The chip writes one. Each passed alone,
and together they buzz.

51
00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.160
Linearity is a promise about sums.

52
00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.720
Now add one more property.

53
00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:08.340
A system is time-invariant when delaying
the input only delays the output.

54
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.940
Together they make a law:
a tone that goes into a linear,

55
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.120
time-invariant system comes out at the
same pitch, only bigger or smaller,

56
00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.060
and shifted. No new tones, ever.

57
00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.840
So the fix is to keep every
input inside the range.

58
00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:23.720
The loudest input the booster ever sees,
speakerphones included, is point five.

59
00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:26.180
Times two lands right on
the edge, with no margin.

60
00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.460
Instead, keep the loudest peak at
point eight, a fifth below the edge.

61
00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:30.720
That gap is the headroom.

62
00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:33.580
Point eight divided by
point five is one point six.

63
00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.580
Now the buzz meter, which measures
the tones that weren't in the input,

64
00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.280
reads zero percent on every caller.

65
00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:41.700
Loud callers sound like loud people again.

66
00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.880
Then a new ticket arrives:
I can hear myself.

67
00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:46.540
Every word comes back, faint,
a quarter of a second later.

68
00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:47.560
That's the next bug.
